Track 3: The Sky We Left Under the Rug
- Story & Context: The closing track became a legendary myth aboard the vessel, written as a tribute to the ship's oldest passenger who passed away just three days before reaching the outer orbit of Proxima Centauri B. The band used a glass harmonica and soft yarn mallets on the lower registers of the marimba to simulate the feeling of pulling back a carpet to reveal an entire planet's atmosphere. During the final take, the brass player dropped his mute, but the resulting resonant clatter sounded so much like a ship's anchor dropping that they preserved the error. The song is still played at every arrival anniversary across the outer colonies. "Pull the corner back and tell me if the grass is still green," asks the soothing group vocal harmony as the track fades into cosmic tape hiss.
